<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Five Pillars of Islam</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as arkān al-Islām, arkān ad-dīn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Five Pillars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> are the fundamental practices of Islam, held to be obligatory acts of worship for all Muslims: the shahada (profession of faith), salah (prayer), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakat">zakat</a> (almsgiving), sawm (fasting during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a>) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a> (pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>). Each is alluded to in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">Hadith</a> gives further detail. Comparable practices existed in pre-Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Christians, but the Quran and Hadith gave them a monotheistic centre and tied them to the life of Muhammad. The pillars are particularly emphasised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a>; Shia Muslims also recognise them, though their classification differs, and some communities such as the Alevis reject them.</p></div>
